---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import CelticTool from "../tools/CelticTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="celtic">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        A tree whose branches and roots both run into a plaited Celtic ring, merged with
        <strong class="text-slate-300">real vector booleans</strong> into one closed piece — no raster round trip, no
        seam where two branches cross. Generative knotwork, flowing Bézier limbs, and leaves that are engraved rather
        than cut when they would otherwise be swallowed.
    </Fragment>

    <CelticTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </CelticTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Overlaps are joins, not cuts</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    This is the design that most reliably comes off the bed in pieces, and the reason is always the
                    same. A tree of life is branches crossing a trunk, crossing each other, crossing their own leaves
                    and running into a ring. Drawn honestly as outlines, every one of those crossings is a
                    <em>pair of cut lines through both shapes</em> — so the laser dutifully cuts the branch off the
                    trunk on its way past, and the piece arrives as confetti.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    So every limb, leaf, strand and tab is turned into a polygon and the lot is merged with a
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">true boolean union</strong>. What comes back is one outline and the
                    holes in it: branch meets trunk with no seam, leaf overlaps twig with no seam, and the only closed
                    curves in the export are the real edge of the real piece and the real openwork between the
                    branches. The status bar counts them, and it counts one of each rather than one per crossing.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    It used to do that by painting the drawing onto a canvas and tracing the bitmap. That worked, and
                    it cost the one thing a cut file is for. A traced edge is a staircase at whatever resolution the
                    canvas was, every curve came back with a few hundred points, and the whole design went through a
                    raster round trip on its way to a vector file. There is no canvas in it now — and the status bar
                    reports how long the booleans took, because that is the honest price of doing it properly.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The knot is a plan, not a weave</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Celtic knotwork is strands passing over and under each other, and a cut piece is flat: there is no
                    over. So what the frame actually draws is the braid's <em>plan</em> — three sinusoidal strands
                    sweeping in and out across the band, a third of a wavelength apart, unioned so that every crossing
                    is a join rather than a pair of cuts. Knot density is how many loops they make round the circle;
                    the braid gap is the whitespace between them, which on a cut piece is the hole.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    A thin rim closes each edge of the band, and it is structural rather than trim. Without it the
                    braid is a scalloped ring whose gaps open inwards and outwards — notches rather than holes, and a
                    row of bumps where the outside edge of the disc should be. With it every gap becomes a real
                    cutout, the disc has a round edge, and the inner rim is what the branches and roots land on. That
                    last part is the whole structure: the tool tells you in the status bar whether the drawing really
                    did merge into a single piece, because that is the one fault the canvas cannot show you.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Density has a ceiling and it follows the circumference rather than being a number somebody picked.
                    A strand sweeps the whole band twice per loop, so past a point the sweep is steeper than the strand
                    is wide, consecutive passes merge, and the whitespace closes up into a plain band. The tool holds
                    it there and says so.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Overlapping leaves are engraved, not cut</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Merged into the union, a leaf lying across a branch or across the leaf beside it loses its own
                        outline — the file gets a lump where the drawing had two leaves, and a full canopy turns into a
                        blob. So every leaf is measured against what is already on the sheet, and one that has lost too
                        much of itself is moved to a green engraved layer instead: you still see every leaf, the detail
                        survives, and no second cut line runs through the twig it hangs off. Leaves are also spread
                        out rather than piled up, because twigs arrive in bunches and drawing every candidate is what
                        makes them clump.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">There is a floor under the leaf size</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A tree with a hundred tiny leaves looks wonderful on screen and comes out of a laser as a
                        hundred identical scorch marks, because each leaf is smaller than the kerf plus the char around
                        it. The beam is about a tenth of a millimetre and so is the burn it leaves. So the floor is
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">enforced rather than suggested</strong>: ask for 2 mm leaves and
                        you get 4 mm leaves and a note saying why. The same reasoning is behind the thinnest-twig
                        figure — each level of branching keeps about 74 % of its parent's width, so depth is what makes
                        twigs delicate, and the number tells you before the piece does.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">What comes out of it</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    A hairline: 0.1 mm stroke, no fill, and every hole written as a subpath of the same path under the
                    even-odd rule, so a cutout stays a cutout in anything that opens the file. Nothing in the export
                    depends on winding order, which is the one thing a boolean library is entitled to change. The
                    engraved leaves are their own green layer, and the optional tabs, feet and backing disc are their
                    own files.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Nothing in the drawing is allowed outside the circle, and that is a property of how the geometry is
                    built rather than a clip taken afterwards: a limb stops half a band inside the rim, the braid's
                    crest touches the outer circle exactly, and a leaf that would cross it is dropped. If any of that
                    ever stops being true, the tool measures it and says so instead of quietly shaving the bulge off.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
